#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205125 :tutorial/content "Given a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, we know that its variance, which we may denote as $\\sigma_x^2$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma_x^2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline x$ is the mean of $x$.\n\nThe summation term\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nis known as a **sum of squares** and is denoted $S_{xx}$. As we'll see, this quantity often appears in statistical analysis, so giving it a separate notation is useful.\n\nSimilarly, if we have a data set $y_1, y_2, \\ldots, y_n$, then we define\n\n$$\nS_{yy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\overline y)^2\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we have the bivariate data set\n$(x_1, y_1), \\qquad (x_2, y_2),\\qquad \\ldots, \\qquad (x_n, y_n)$.\nThen, the covariance is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)(y_i - \\overline y)\n$$\n\nand we may define $S_{xy}$ as\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y})\n$$\n\nFinally, we can define the variance and covariance in terms of $S_{xx}, S_{yy}$, and $S_{xy}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ_{x}^{2} &= \\frac{S_{xx}}{n}, σ_{y}^{2} = \\frac{S_{yy}}{n}, Cov(x, y) = \\frac{S_{xy}}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7e454c5-fb32-5adc-b4bd-0eb93ccd4ce7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10575 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205126 :tutorial/content "Calculating $S_{xx}, S_{yy}$, and $S_{xy}$ using their definitions can be cumbersome. Thankfully, we have the following shortcut formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xx} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2} \\\\\nS_{yy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i})^{2} \\\\\nS_{xy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}y_{i} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})(∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "c497f58a-e56b-5fde-9a53-c8553e8dcbcf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10576 :tutorial/title "Shortcut Formulas"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205148 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove that\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = ∑_(i)x_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of $S_{xx}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xx} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the binomial:\n\n$$\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i}^{2} - 2x_{i} \\cdot x^(―) + x^(―)^{2})\n$$\n\nDistributing the summation, we have\n\n$$\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)2x_{i} \\cdot x^(―) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x^(―)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, since $2, \\overline x$, and $\\overline{x}^2$ are constants, we can write this as\n\n$$\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - 2x^(―) \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + x^(―)^{2} \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)1\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = n\\overline x, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^n 1 = n\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xx} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - 2x^(―) \\cdot nx^(―) + x^(―)^{2} \\cdot n \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - 2nx^(―)^{2} + nx^(―)^{2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - nx^(―)^{2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - n \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - n \\cdot \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot (∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i)x_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThe proofs for $S_{yy}$ and $S_{xy}$ are similar." :tutorial/id #uuid "7dab2448-da48-5100-9373-89c074ae5326" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10852 :tutorial/title "Proving the Shortcut Formulas"}]}
